<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latin Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1204">1204</a>–<a href="/years/1261">1261</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Latin Empire of Constantinople, Empire of Romania, Constantinopolitan Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Latin Empire, officially the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, was a feudal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader state</a> founded by the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> on lands captured from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, which lasted from 1204 to 1261. After the crusaders plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in April 1204, they chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor">Baldwin IX of Flanders</a> as emperor and divided Byzantine territory into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states, with a Catholic emperor in place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> one. Challenged by the Byzantine rump states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and at constant war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, it declined steadily, and in 1261 the Nicene Empire recovered Constantinople under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>. The last emperor, Baldwin II, went into exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> had been called to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, but it led to the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The crusaders had planned to restore the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a>, who had been displaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a>, and had been promised financial and military aid by Isaac's son Alexios IV. When they were not paid, they captured and plundered the city in April 1204.</p><p>In the Partitio terrarum imperii Romaniae, signed on 1 October 1204, three eighths of the empire, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and other islands, went to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and the Latin Empire claimed the rest, divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> such as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Baldwin was elected emperor on 9 May 1204 with Venetian support and crowned on 16 May in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, in a ceremony that followed Eastern Roman practice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> the crusaders took most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> by 1205 and, by the Treaty of Nymphaeum in 1214, held most of Bithynia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> defeat at Poimanenon in 1224, Emperor Robert of Courtenay ceded his Asian possessions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, and in 1235 the last fell. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> crushed the Latin cavalry at the Battle of Adrianople on 14 April 1205 and captured Baldwin, who died in captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>.</p><p>The Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1224 and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in 1225–26, appearing before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Baldwin II took over in 1237 a much-diminished state, and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> seeking aid, largely without success. On the night of 24 to 25 July 1261 the Nicaean general Alexios Strategopoulos, with a force of 800, found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> garrison and the Venetian fleet away raiding and entered the city through a secret passage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The empire was run on Western European feudal principles with some elements of the Byzantine bureaucracy. A council of barons and the Venetian podestà of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> had a major voice in governance, and the podestà, practically independent of the emperor, held the title Governor of One-Fourth and One-Half of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. A Latin Catholic hierarchy was set up.</p><p>Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> who accepted papal supremacy could keep their sees, and the Greek clergy kept elements of its rite, including a married priesthood, though in a subordinate position. After 1261 Baldwin II and his successors kept the imperial title, which passed through the Angevin princes of Taranto until James of Baux inherited it in 1373.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Latin Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1204 the crusaders sacked Constantinople and established a <b>Latin Empire</b> that held the city until 1261.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The principality’s strength rested on mounted knights who held fiefs in return for military service, on a network of castles and on the support of Venice, the <b>Latin Empire</b> and later the Angevin kings of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…greatest victory came on 14 April 1205 at the Battle of Adrianople, when Kaloyan, allied with Greek nobles of Thrace who resented Latin rule, drew the knights of the new <b>Latin Empire</b> into pursuit and destroyed their army; Emperor Baldwin I was captured and died in Bulgarian captivity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They set up a <b>Latin Empire</b>, and Byzantium never recovered its former strength.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin's <b>Latin Empire</b> had been founded by the Fourth Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexios Strategopoulos, a general of the Empire of Nicaea, led Nicaean forces to recapture Constantinople, held by the <b>Latin Empire</b> since the Fourth Crusade of 1204.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
